  "bibliography": {
    "abstract": "Community-based events are increasingly recognised as important yet underexplored spaces for informal environmental learning, particularly in rapidly urbanising cities facing climate change pressures. This study examines how a community fair functions as an informal learning space for urban greenery and climate change adaptation, drawing on evidence from the 2025 Flower Community Fair in Accra, Ghana. Using a mixed-methods approach combining attendee surveys (n = 189) and qualitative insights from observations and open-ended responses, the study analyses participation patterns, perceived relevance of home gardening, and reported post-event adaptation intentions. Findings indicate high levels of perceived relevance and strong expressed intentions to engage in home gardening among participants. However, given the self-selected nature of attendees and the absence of longitudinal follow-up, the results reflect stated intentions rather than confirmed behavioural change. The study provides insight into how community-based events can support the translation of existing environmental interest into practical adaptation-oriented intentions within urban contexts.",
